<?nvs
// The memory cap, as a program: a script that asks for more than the
// operator granted is terminated, and the termination is a `FATAL`.
//
// ADR 0020 is why there is no `try` anywhere below. A limit breach never
// reaches an ordinary `catch` — it goes to `Core\Fatal::onLimit` if one is
// registered and to the ladder otherwise — so a version of this program
// wrapping the loop in a `catch` would be asserting the opposite of the
// rule. The one line on stdout is what proves the process was *running*
// when the cap fired rather than refused before it started.
//
// `[limits] memory = "256M"` in this repository's own `nvs.toml` is the
// cap, so the loop below asks for twice it.
//
// The loop is bounded on purpose, and the bound is the fixture's own
// safety rather than part of what it pins. An unbounded `while (true)`
// here would, in a tree where the cap is not yet enforced, allocate until
// the machine running the acceptance check gave out; bounded, that tree
// exits 0 with nothing on stderr and the check fails in the ordinary way
// instead. 512 slabs of 1 MiB is comfortably past a 256M cap and
// comfortably short of anything a developer machine minds.
//
// stdout, exit status and stderr for this program are frozen by a check in a
// goal record under `data/goals/`; the source is not.

echo 'before the cap', "\n";

array<string> $ballast = [];
int $slabs = 0;

while ($slabs < 512) {
    $ballast[] = Core\Str::repeat('x', 1048576);
    $slabs++;
}

// Only reachable in a tree that is not enforcing the cap, which is what
// makes the check's `exit = "nonzero"` half worth asserting.
echo 'held ' . Core\Arr::count($ballast) . ' slabs, uncapped', "\n";
